this is the gate of Europe it gazes  southward from the Italian island of  Lampedusa from here it's 300 kilometers  to Libya the artwork was created in  honor of the migrants who lost their  lives crossing the Mediterranean seeking  European shores at the port there's a  graveyard for their boats these are  fishing boats from Tunisia Libya and  Egypt after having discharged their  human cargo on the island they are now  of rotting on the shores of Lampedusa up  the road from the port there's this  house its owners have decorated the  facade with colorful planks from ships  it's another tribute to the migrants and  here  a man has collected possessions from  aboard the migrant ships there are  photos baby bottles and cigarettes  Giacomo Sveta la Sol built up this  collection over several years he found  the objects both inside the ships and on  the dumping grounds of La Medusa he  wants to help preserve the memory of  those who arrived here doing them  justice by calling the migrants or worse  illegals they're not defined by being  migrants you see they eat listen to  music brush their teeth wear perfume  steadily so believes his collection  makes a political statement about  Europe's policies on migration he hopes  that it will draw attention to why  people make such a perilous journey  politicians have chosen to deal with  migrants coming from a place where  there's war or persecution in this way  they've never tried to regulate migrants  journeys here it is the system that  ensures that these people continue to  arrive illegally transient migrant still  brave that journey but fewer now arrive  in Lampedusa due to the Italian Navy  operation called  mara Nostrum the sea is ours sailors  pick up migrants off the Libyan coast  and then take them directly to the  Italian mainland but locals like Pietro  Bartolo will never forget what happened  here in the past off the shores of the  island when they started the rescue they  found very few of them was still afloat  I remember the first day there were 111  bodies they were lined up here along  this gangway it was truly horrifying  October 3rd 2013  a ship with five hundred migrants on  board capsized off Lampedusa most were  from Eritrea but they were also  Somalians and people from other African  countries on board 366 people drowned  I've never seen anything of that  magnitude 366 dead the scene filled me  with rage they had almost made it here  there were just 200 meters from the  coast you can imagine them gazing out at  the island and thinking about arriving  but they didn't make it here by a few  metres the doctor has set up an art  exhibition here a year after the tragedy  the disaster was a shock for locals and  it ruined lumpy News's reputation for  many Europeans the island will always be  associated with migrants drowning at sea  but many on the island think it's time  to move on John Damiano Lombardo henslin  produces Hotel Association he prefers to  show visitors the other side of the  Mediterranean island is gorgeous here it  boasts every type of fish from great  white sharks to whales dolphins and sea  turtles so this is the other side an  island with so many resources so much  potential you could rebuild the  archipelago's brand on that but for now  blue seas and golden sand are not enough  to lure the tourists 40% fewer are  vacationing here this year the industry  blames the migrant crisis for that  even if it's now playing out far away  from the cliffs of Lampedusa these  visitors aren't here for tourism they're  survivors of the disaster a year ago  they've come from Sweden and Norway  where they now live to remember those  aboard their doomed ship I remember all  at that time at that moment it's bad for  me and I remember all sounds cries  shouts these people's story has defined  an island and has come to symbolize  Europe's migration crisis a year on hope  and despair still mixed together in the  waters off Lampedusa
 

